Fixes #74.

**What.** With `backend = "iptables-nft"` and `docker_coexistence`, a Docker restart put `DOCKER-USER` / `DOCKER-FORWARD` above `PARALLAX_FORWARD`. Parallax's own `-i wg0 -j ACCEPT` in `DOCKER-USER` then accepted tunnel traffic before the tiers. Apply only checked that the jump was present, and drift detection didn't look at order. The nftables backend is unchanged.

**How.**
- `DOCKER-USER` (iptables) starts with `-j PARALLAX_FORWARD`, followed only by the `-o <tunnel>` accepts. `PARALLAX_FORWARD` gives every packet from a tunnel its verdict, so the `-i` accepts were never reached; they would only have been a bypass if the jump went missing.
- `apply::decides_first`: each `PARALLAX_*` jump (all four hooks) must be present once and be the first rule of its chain, holds aside. In FORWARD it also counts when Docker's `DOCKER-USER` jump is first and starts with ours. This way the daemon and Docker don't keep reordering each other.
- The holds (#56), hook moves, `DOCKER-USER` refill and hold lift are each one `iptables-restore --noflush` transaction, so no packet finds a jump missing. Holds always go to the top of their chain, `DOCKER-USER`'s too. The order is holds → restore → nat hooks → `DOCKER-USER` → filter hooks → lift.
- Drift: an out-of-order live state always counts as drift, and so does `DOCKER-USER` appearing or going.
- Teardown removes `DOCKER-USER`'s rules last-first. ADR 0003 and design.md are updated.

**Left open:** from Docker starting until the next drift check (60 s), whatever Docker's own chains accept (published container ports) comes first. That can't be closed while filtering lives in filter FORWARD; it's documented in ADR 0003.

**Tested.**
- fmt, clippy, `cargo test --workspace`; netns ALL PASS in root and unit mode on the MacBook.
- A scratch netns check on iptables-nft 1.8.13 with a real dockerd 29.9.1 in the gateway namespace: start, two Docker restarts, `DOCKER-FORWARD` first, a network create and a fresh `DOCKER-USER`. A denied port stayed denied throughout, with one re-apply per restart (Docker's nat rules) and none after. Re-apply left the rules unchanged and teardown left nothing.
- The same with fake Docker chains, including one that accepts everything.

**Review (Codex `gpt-6.2-sol` high, Gemini `gemini-4-neon` high, two rounds).** Taken:
- one transaction per change (delete-then-insert gaps in the jump moves and the `DOCKER-USER` refresh);
- holds at the top, also in `DOCKER-USER` (Docker getting above the holds mid-apply; retries only `-C`-checking holds);
- an out-of-order state is never a drift baseline;
- no `-i` accepts in iptables `DOCKER-USER`;
- teardown deletes last-first;
- the "holds stay" note on every failure after the holds, but not on a failed lift;
- duplicate jumps cleaned up;
- drift sees `DOCKER-USER` come and go;
- the lift in one transaction;
- an `iptables-save` that won't run counts as out of order;
- tests for teardown and `save_table`.

Declined:
- the Docker-startup window (above);
- scoping `PARALLAX_FORWARD`'s reply/related accepts to tunnels (existing iptables behaviour, not about order);
- `DOCKER-FORWARD` first during a restore (Docker's chains only accept flows to containers, which don't use Parallax's NAT);
- a netns case for the iptables backend (follow-up).

## O que muda

No macOS e no Linux, onde não há pacote nativo verificável, o MySQL e o MariaDB passam a ser preparados em container, do mesmo jeito que o PostgreSQL (#165, #166):

- **Onde vale:**
  - MariaDB no macOS;
  - MySQL e MariaDB no Linux ARM;
  - sistemas que não atendem ao pacote nativo (glibc antiga, macOS anterior ao 15, libaio ausente).

  No Windows e onde há pacote nativo, nada muda.
- **Imagens:** `mysql:9.7.4-oraclelinux9` e `mariadb:12.3.3-noble`, as imagens oficiais do Docker, fixadas pelo digest do índice (amd64 e arm64). Depois do download, o app confere que a cópia do motor tem esse digest. O MariaDB está na 12.3.3 porque a imagem oficial da 12.3.4 ainda não existe.
- **Senha e isolamento:**
  - a senha inicial do root vai por um `--env-file` com permissão `0600`, apagado logo depois, inclusive se a gravação falhar;
  - em seguida ela é trocada, em `root@'%'` e `root@'localhost'`, pela senha guardada no cofre;
  - a porta fica só em 127.0.0.1;
  - o usuário do projeto é `'u'@'%'`, com direitos só no próprio banco.
- **Confirmação:** "Preparar {motor} para este projeto?", com download e espaço medidos:
  - MySQL: cerca de 250 MB de download e 1,1 GB em disco;
  - MariaDB: cerca de 100 MB e 450 MB.
- **Iguais ao PostgreSQL:** andamento no cartão, falhas com o nome do motor, "Criar {motor} novo", e o fluxo sem motor ou com o motor parado.
- **Documentação:** Ajuda, README (PT/EN), DATABASES.md, README-MACOS.txt e CHANGELOG.

## Testes

- `cargo fmt`, `clippy` e `cargo test` passam, com testes novos no runtime e no desktop.
- No CI, o `validate-sql-server` vira uma matriz com o ciclo real em Docker para SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL e MariaDB. Ele confere:
  - imagem com o digest fixado;
  - porta só em 127.0.0.1;
  - nenhum arquivo de senha sobrando e senha inicial trocada;
  - projeto conectado e senha errada recusada;
  - outro banco recusado, já que o usuário só tem direitos no próprio;
  - parar confirmado pelo `inspect`, reiniciar e entrar de novo.

