## What

When a node never joins and its private address answered port 22 `open` in #606's probe from the k3s server, the node is now read from the inside at the join deadline. That means one ssh to its private address, hopping through the k3s server, with a 25 s bound (`src/lib/deploy/k8s/node-join-read.js`).

- **The hop.** It is a `ProxyCommand` that runs the command's own `ssh -i <key> <host-key opts> -W %h:%p root@<k3s server>`. `-J` is not used, because command-line `-i`/`-o` options never reach a ProxyJump hop.
  - The outer hop pins the k3s server in the env's known_hosts, exactly as today.
  - The inner hop pins the node in a throwaway known_hosts with `accept-new`, in a `vc-node-read-` temp dir that is removed afterwards. It also runs with `ControlMaster=no`/`ControlPath=none`. VPC addresses repeat across clusters (10.159.218.4 was two servers in one run), so a pinned entry would break a later read, and a shared mux socket could reach another cluster's server.
  - Every `%` in a ProxyCommand token is doubled, so the mux path's `%C` reaches the outer ssh as written. `$SHELL` is `/bin/sh` for the ProxyCommand.
  - I checked locally that real OpenSSH 9.6 splits the ProxyCommand into exactly the outer argv. The check used a stub `ssh` on PATH, so nothing connected.
- **What it reads.** Each fact gets one `[node-join] on the node itself: <label>: <fact>` line:
  - the boot marks in `/var/log/vcboot`;
  - `cloud-init status` and its JSON errors;
  - the interfaces' state and IPv4 addresses (`ip -br link`, `ip -br -4 addr`);
  - the default IPv4 routes;
  - `networkctl list`;
  - a 3 s TCP connect to 1.1.1.1:443;
  - a 3 s `getent hosts get.k3s.io`;
  - `curl -sS -o /dev/null -m 5 -w '%{http_code}' https://get.k3s.io`, recording the code or curl's error;
  - a 3 s GET of `http://169.254.169.254/`, recording only whether it answered and the status code, never the body;
  - at most 10 failure lines from `/var/log/cloud-init-output.log`.
- **The sentence.** One sentence read from those facts is appended to the node's line in the failure:
  - no interface holds the public address;
  - there is no default route;
  - the default route leaves by the private (or another) interface;
  - the address and route are set up and its traffic goes unanswered;
  - it reaches the internet but get.k3s.io does not resolve or does not answer;
  - outbound works, and the boot stopped, is still running, never reached mark `k`, or finished, with the failure line.

  "Stopped" and "never" are used only when cloud-init says the boot is over.
- **Callers.** Both callers that probe now read: the deploy (`deployK3sSteps`) and the failover's worker wait. The read is a `readNode` option on `clusterProbe`, so `awaitNodesJoined` is unchanged. It runs only on a positive `port 22 open`. A missing, failed or timed-out probe result is never read.

## Why

Vultr run 45994716507 (14:21Z) was the first failure with #606's probe. The standby's database node answered `ping 2/2, port 22 open, neighbour DELAY` on 10.159.218.4 from the k3s server and nothing on 66.42.120.170. Every ssh from the runner timed out on the public address, and the node never registered.

That is the fourth database node lost while its provider said it was running: Hetzner fsn1 2026-11-06, Hetzner nbg1 2026-11-27, and Vultr ord in runs 45956890734 and 45994716507. From outside, three causes look the same:

- (a) a public interface with no address or route;
- (b) a provider network that passes nothing;
- (c) a boot script that stopped for its own reason.

The rig is destroyed at teardown, so the reading has to be taken on the failure path. This is diagnosis only: no retry, reboot or replacement. A read that fails is one log line, and the NodesNeverJoinedError is unchanged.

## Redaction (the main review point)

Everything the read prints goes through `redactNodeRead` before any of it is parsed or logged:

- every line containing `password` (any case) is dropped;
- each occurrence of the cluster's k3s token is replaced. The deploy passes `infraOutputs.k3sToken` and the failover passes the standby stack's `up.outputs.k3sToken`;
- anything matching `K10[0-9a-f]{20,}` is replaced, together with its `::user:password` tail;
- credential-shaped assignments, bearer headers and URL userinfo are replaced (`redactCredentials`).

The run's stdout is marked `secret`, so `runCommandAsync` never echoes a failed read raw under CI. The lines printed before a timeout are still kept, through `onStdoutLine`. The script itself holds no secret, runs `exec 2>/dev/null` first, and only runs read-only commands.

## Tests

- `tests/unit/deploy/node-join-read.test.ts` (new):
  - the script, run only under stub `ip`, `curl`, `getent`, `timeout`, `cloud-init` and `networkctl` with its own boot and output logs;
  - the parser;
  - the redaction, including the token, K10 shapes and password lines;
  - each sentence chosen from its facts, with no em or en dashes;
  - the argv: the outer hop's key and known_hosts in the ProxyCommand, and the inner throwaway known_hosts, which is removed after both success and failure;
  - the ProxyCommand split by a real `/bin/sh` into exactly the outer argv;
  - the 25 s bound, and a timed-out read keeping its lines;
  - `clusterProbe` reading only the nodes whose private port 22 was `open`;
  - a failing read leaving the `NodesNeverJoinedError` byte-identical.
- `tests/unit/deploy/node-join-census.test.ts`: every `clusterProbe` call passes `readNode`, built by `nodeRead` with the `secrets` it redacts, or is listed in `READ_EXEMPT` (empty). Mutation-checked on both callers.
- `temp-dir-census`: lists the `vc-node-read-` prefix, which holds no secret.
- `ssh-host-key-pinning`: covers the new file.
- `printed-url-link-census`: lists the two plain `https://get.k3s.io` reads, one in the thrown error's sentence and one in the stderr fact line.
- Local gate: `pnpm test:unit` 1163 files, 17894 passed, 1 expected fail, 1 skipped. Integration: 488 passed, 166 skipped. `pnpm lint` and `pnpm unused` are clean.

